# How the repository stores text, decided here instead of by whoever cloned it.
#
# There was no .gitattributes at all, so every clone's line endings were decided by its own
# `core.autocrlf`. Checked before writing this: the index is **815 LF, 104 binary, 11 empty and
# zero CRLF**, so what is stored is already right and `core.autocrlf=true` is what puts CRLF in
# this working tree on checkout. (An earlier reading of "726 files with CRLF" was of the working
# tree, not the repository - worth recording, because the two look identical from `grep` and only
# one of them is a problem.)
#
# What this file buys is that the answer stops depending on a per-clone setting: `text=auto
# eol=lf` normalises on commit and checks out LF everywhere, so a contributor whose git is
# configured differently cannot flip 800 files in one diff, and CI, the Docker build and this
# machine all read the same bytes.
#
# The two rules that are not merely explicitness are at the bottom: `.ps1` and `.bat` need CRLF
# or Windows will not run them, and the Makefile's tab-indented recipes must never be filtered.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Files whose bytes are their content. Marked explicitly rather than left to Git's heuristic,
# because a heuristic that guesses wrong on one file corrupts it silently.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.bmp binary
*.tif binary
*.tiff binary
*.pdf binary
*.pt binary
*.pth binary
*.onnx binary
*.npy binary
*.npz binary
*.parquet binary
*.joblib binary
*.pkl binary
*.7z binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.ico binary
*.ttf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

# Windows needs CRLF in these two or they do not run.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# A tab-indented recipe line is the Makefile's syntax; never let a filter touch it.
Makefile text eol=lf
